10:13:35 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: First-person shooters usually handle bullets using a combination of **physics/collision detection** and **network synchronization**. ### 1. Hitscan vs. physical projectiles Many weapons do not simulate a literal bullet flying through the air. - **Hitscan weapons** (common for rifles, pistols, sniper rifles) fire an invisible ray from the gun in the aiming direction. - The game checks what that ray intersects first: a wall, object, or player hitbox. - This happens effectively instantly, which is why shots appear to land immediately. Other weapons use actual simulated projectiles: - Rockets, grenades, arrows, and sometimes bullets have a position, velocity, gravity, drag, and travel time. - Each frame or simulation “tick,” the server moves the projectile forward and checks whether its path crossed anything. - To prevent a fast projectile from passing through a target between frames, games often test the whole segment from its old position to its new position, rather than only checking its final location. ### 2. Players have collision shapes and hitboxes A character model may look detailed, but the game generally uses simpler invisible shapes for collision: - Capsules or boxes for the body - Separate hitboxes for head, torso, arms, and legs - Environment collision meshes for walls, floors, and cover When a bullet ray or projectile intersects one of those shapes, the game determines the hit location and applies the appropriate damage. ### 3. The server is usually authoritative In an online game, the client—the player’s computer or console—typically sends information such as: - “I fired at this time” - “My weapon was aimed in this direction” - “I was at this position” The **server** verifies the shot. It performs the collision test, decides whether it hit, calculates damage, and informs all players of the result. This prevents players from simply telling the server, “I hit that enemy,” which would be easy to cheat. ### 4. Lag compensation makes shooting feel responsive Internet delay means that by the time the server receives a firing message, the target may have moved from where the shooter saw them. To compensate, many games use **lag compensation** or **server-side rewind**: 1. The server stores a short history of player positions and hitboxes. 2. When it receives a shot, it estimates when the player fired based on network latency. 3. It temporarily checks the target’s hitbox at the position they had at that earlier moment. 4. It tests the bullet ray against that historical position. This is why a shot can register even if, from the target’s perspective, they had just moved behind cover. On the shooter’s screen, they may still have been visible when the shot was fired. ### 5. Prediction and interpolation hide network delay Games also make movement appear smooth through: - **Client-side prediction:** your own machine immediately shows your firing animation, recoil, muzzle flash, and sometimes an estimated hit marker before server confirmation. - **Interpolation:** other players’ movement is displayed slightly in the past using smoothly blended server updates, reducing jitter. - **Reconciliation:** if the server later disagrees with the client’s prediction, the client corrects its state. So, in real time, the player sees an immediate response, while the server quietly verifies what actually happened. In short: a shooter usually represents a shot as either a fast collision ray or a simulated moving object, checks it against simplified player hitboxes and map geometry, and relies on server authority plus lag compensation to decide fair online results.
