10:13:47 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Yes—at a basic level, a hitscan shot is still evaluating the bullet’s potential path through the world. The difference is *how* it is represented and when the calculation occurs. For a hitscan weapon, the game usually does something like: 1. Take the gun/camera position and aim direction. 2. Apply weapon spread or recoil, if applicable. 3. Cast a line or ray outward to a maximum range. 4. Find the first object or hitbox the line intersects. 5. Register the hit immediately. So it is calculating a path, but it does **not** continuously track a bullet object over time. There is no bullet position at 1 ms, then 2 ms, then 3 ms, etc. Instead, it asks a geometric question all at once: > “Along this line, what would the bullet hit first?” A physical projectile instead has ongoing state, such as: - position - velocity - direction - gravity/drop - air resistance - remaining lifetime - possibly ricochet or penetration behavior The game updates that state repeatedly at simulation ticks. A hitscan weapon can still have more complex “trajectory-like” behavior. For example: - Bullet penetration may cast another ray after passing through a surface. - Ricochets may calculate a reflected direction and cast another ray. - A weapon with bullet drop may use several short ray segments approximating a curved path. - Lag compensation can test the ray against historical player hitbox positions. But in the simplest case, hitscan is best thought of as an **instant line-of-sight collision test**, rather than a bullet being simulated and tracked while it travels.
