10:14:03 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re right. Games such as **PUBG** and many **Battlefield** weapons use simulated ballistic projectiles rather than simple hitscan rays. The bullet has travel time, velocity, gravity drop, often drag, and sometimes penetration or ricochet behavior. The game tracks it as a lightweight physics object, typically something like: ```text bullet: position velocity mass / ballistic parameters age owner / weapon data ``` At each simulation tick—commonly tens of times per second, sometimes more—the server advances the bullet: ```text newVelocity = oldVelocity + gravity * dt newPosition = oldPosition + newVelocity * dt ``` It then checks the path segment from `oldPosition` to `newPosition` against the world and player hitboxes. This segment test is important: a bullet may travel several meters in one tick, so checking only its position at discrete instants could let it pass through a target or wall. For a more realistic model, drag may reduce velocity: ```text velocity += gravity * dt velocity -= dragFunction(velocity) * dt position += velocity * dt ``` The game detects the earliest collision along that segment. If it hits: - a player hitbox, it determines body part and damage; - terrain or a wall, it stops, embeds, ricochets, or penetrates depending on material and bullet properties; - nothing, it continues into the next tick. Online play adds the networking part: - Your client usually displays the shot immediately for responsiveness. - The server simulates or validates the projectile and decides official hits. - The server sends state updates or impact events to clients. - Other clients interpolate those updates so the projectile and impacts appear smooth. - Depending on the game, the server may compensate for latency when deciding the initial shot, but a projectile still has to travel through space after firing. So your observation is accurate: a visible, dropping, delayed bullet is generally being tracked as a projectile. The important distinction is not “whether a path is calculated,” but whether the game evaluates that path instantly as a ray or advances a projectile through time.
