10:14:35 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It is standard for shooters to calculate **where a shot goes and what it hits**. But it is not standard for every shooter to continuously simulate and track every bullet as a time-moving object. The terminology can make this sound like a bigger divide than it is: - **Hitscan:** calculates the shot’s path immediately as a line/ray and determines the first collision. - **Projectile ballistics:** calculates the path over time, updating position and velocity as the bullet travels. Both involve a direction, range, collision checks, and often weapon spread. So in that broad sense, yes: shooters generally track or evaluate a shot’s trajectory. But games choose different levels of simulation depending on their goals: | Game style | Common approach | Why | |---|---|---| | Arcade shooters / close-range arena shooters | Mostly hitscan | Immediate, responsive, simpler to network | | Tactical or mil-sim shooters | Ballistic projectiles | Travel time, leading targets, bullet drop, realism | | Large-map shooters such as PUBG | Projectile simulation | Long distances make velocity and gravity meaningful | | Battlefield | Often projectile-based for bullets, explosives, vehicles, etc.; details vary by title and weapon | Supports range, drop, and large-scale combat | | Hybrid games | Mix of both | Different weapon classes can behave differently | Also, a visible tracer does not necessarily prove that the game simulates every visible bullet independently. Some games render tracer effects cosmetically while the actual hit detection uses a simpler calculation. Conversely, a game may simulate a bullet but render no visible tracer. So the general rule is: > Nearly all shooters calculate a shot path and collision. > Only some simulate that path as a bullet moving through the world over time. For games like PUBG—and many Battlefield weapons—the second model is indeed central to gameplay.
